tests: collect garbage before tpool.killall() in the test cleanup

The cleanup of the base TestCase (`_common_cleanup` in
cinder/tests/unit/test.py) calls `tpool.killall()` after every test, so
every test that uses eventlet's native thread pool starts its 20 threads
again in `tpool.setup()`. The Ceph backup driver tests
(cinder/tests/unit/backup/drivers/test_backup_ceph.py) leave os-brick
`RBDVolumeIOWrapper` objects to the garbage collector, some with an
`eventlet.tpool.Proxy` as their image. The wrapper is an `io.RawIOBase`
and closes itself on finalization: `close()` calls `flush()`, `flush()`
calls `self.rbd_image.require_not_closed()`
(os_brick/initiator/linuxrbd.py), and the proxy runs that through
`tpool.execute()`.

A cyclic collection that finalizes such a wrapper during a
`tpool.setup()` hangs the stestr worker for good, in one of two ways:

- On the thread in `setup()`, while its loop starts the pool threads:
  `execute()` finds `_setup_already` set, queues the flush and waits for
  the result, but `tpool_trampoline`, which delivers results, is spawned
  only after that loop.
- On a pool thread still in `Thread._bootstrap`: the flush waits on an
  eventlet hub of that native thread's own, which nothing ever wakes, so
  the thread never sets `_started` and `Thread.start()` in `setup()`
  waits forever.

gdb, py-spy and faulthandler dumps of hung workers show both stacks.
The worker stops after about 500 tests, in the backup driver tests,
while the other workers finish, and `test-service-images` then runs
into its 90-minute timeout: for cinder 2026.2 in 6 of 48 runs between
2026-10-06 and 2026-10-22 (the last in run 39784849996), for 2026.1 in
3 of 91 runs since 2026-09-21 (run 38241162511 shows the same stalled
worker). Run locally with four workers, the 29.0.0 suite hung in 5 of
about 45 runs.

The patch runs `gc.collect()` in the cleanup, before `tpool.killall()`,
whenever the test started the pool. Whatever the test left to the
garbage collector is finalized there, on the test's own thread while
the pool and its trampoline still run, so the flush completes and no
such wrapper is left for a later `setup()`. Tests that never start the
pool skip the collection. With a hook that logs every eventlet hub
switch inside a collection, all wrapper finalizations of a patched
29.0.0 run happen inside that `gc.collect()`, and the patched suite hung
in none of 36 runs. The collections cost time: the whole 29.0.0 suite
took 448 s with the patch and 397 s without, on four cores, one run
each.

Closing every wrapper the Ceph tests build, directly or through the
driver, would fix the leak at its source, but the next such leak would
deadlock the same way.

The patch changes test code only, so it changes nothing at runtime and
tests/container-images/verify_cinder.sh needs no assertion.
Its 2026.1 twin,
patches/cinder/2026.1/0005-tests-collect-garbage-before-tpool-killall.patch,
carries the same hunks, cut from 28.0.0.

Applies to cinder 29.0.0 (2026.2).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

diff --git a/cinder/tests/unit/test.py b/cinder/tests/unit/test.py
index d7f683f..7fe33ab 100644
--- a/cinder/tests/unit/test.py
+++ b/cinder/tests/unit/test.py
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@ inline callbacks.
 
 """
 import copy
+import gc
 import logging
 import os
 from unittest import mock
@@ -345,6 +346,13 @@ class TestCase(testtools.TestCase):
         if not issues.TPOOL_KILLALL_ISSUE:
             # Ensure we have the default tpool size value and we don't carry
             # threads from other test runs.
+            if tpool._setup_already:
+                # Finalize what this test left to the garbage collector while
+                # the pool still runs: a collected os-brick
+                # RBDVolumeIOWrapper flushes through its tpool.Proxy, and a
+                # collection inside the next tpool.setup() would wait for
+                # that flush forever.
+                gc.collect()
             tpool.killall()
             tpool._nthreads = 20
 
